    "resultsInterpreter": [
      {
        "display": "Benjamin Afolabi, MD"
      }
    ],
    "conclusion": "1. Cholelithiasis without sonographic evidence of acute cholecystitis.\n2. 2.8 cm solid, vascular exophytic mass in the upper pole of the left kidney, concerning for a renal neoplasm. Recommend contrast-enhanced renal mass protocol MRI or CT.",
    "presentedForm": [
      {
        "contentType": "text/plain",
        "text": "EXAM:\nUS ABDOMEN, COMPLETE\n\nCLINICAL INDICATION:\nIntermittent right upper quadrant pain after meals for 3 months. Evaluate for gallstones.\n\nTECHNIQUE:\nReal-time grayscale and color Doppler sonography of the abdomen.\n\nCOMPARISON:\nNone.\n\nFINDINGS:\nLiver: Normal in size (15.2 cm) and echotexture. No focal lesion. Hepatopetal portal venous flow.\nGallbladder: Multiple mobile, shadowing gallstones, the largest measuring 1.4 cm. Wall thickness 2 mm. No pericholecystic fluid. Sonographic Murphy sign is negative.\nBile ducts: Common bile duct measures 4 mm. No intrahepatic biliary dilatation.\nPancreas: Visualized head and body are unremarkable. The tail is obscured by bowel gas.\nSpleen: Normal in size (10.1 cm).\nRight kidney: 10.8 cm. Normal echotexture. No hydronephrosis, stone, or mass.\nLeft kidney: 11.4 cm. 2.8 x 2.5 x 2.6 cm solid, mildly hypoechoic exophytic mass in the upper pole with internal vascularity on color Doppler. No hydronephrosis.\nAorta and IVC: Normal in caliber where visualized.\nOther: No ascites.\n\nIMPRESSION:\n1. Cholelithiasis without sonographic evidence of acute cholecystitis.\n2. 2.8 cm solid, vascular exophytic mass in the upper pole of the left kidney, concerning for a renal neoplasm. Recommend contrast-enhanced renal mass protocol MRI or CT."
      }
    ]
  },
